> and you've seen the Nethack codebase...

I've not looked at that particular messy part of it
for about ten years, and if I intend to keep my
lunch down, I probably won't in the future, either.

After now 42 years of coding, I've learned to
distinguish code that "just works this moment by dint
of plain heroic, if misguided effort" from "code
that works as a target for an ever flowing set of
changes because the design caters for the prospect
of change from the start or after needed refactoring".
That's why I chose to clean up tiles rather than
shovel out the bugs in NetHack as my "no free lunch"
