> No comment. Go away.

No chance. Cope.

Quite some time ago, probably in the late 1970's, I
read an analysis in some long forgotten software
engineering publication, I read many at the time and
since, that it was cheaper and more important,
FASTER, for even a modest sized code change, to make,
every single time you contemplated a change, again,
a pass cleaning up all discernable problems in the
existing code base before making a single one of the
new changes, just because of the needed-effort
multiplying effect of bugs in a code maintenance
target, even if that meant you reformatted the
entire program simply for legibility as part of that
pass.

That has proved more times than I can count since
then to be exactly correct advice.

The idea that letting known problems fester because
they are "too much trouble to fix" is so bogus, it
is a hallmark of the inexperienced and never-to-be
successful software technician; programming is not
a field for the bone lazy.

And if that gouges your particular ox, see "cope",
above.

